From 8457afb0183f25dd512ef1b082fb0cb1902a7f71 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christian Breunig <christian@breunig.cc>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 22:37:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] pptp, l2tp, sstp: re-bind the listening socket on
 configuration reload

All three parsed "bind" and "port" inside their init function and never
looked at them again, so changing the address the server listens on had no
effect until the daemon was restarted - which drops every established
session. Each of them does have a load_config() registered for
EV_CONFIG_RELOAD; it just never touched the socket.

Split the socket setup out of the init path into a <mod>_parse_bind() and a
<mod>_serv_open() pair, and have load_config() compare the newly parsed
address against the one currently bound. Only when it actually differs is a
rebind queued into the server's own context with triton_context_call(),
which is required: the reload handler runs on whichever worker thread went
idle last, not inside a triton context, and only the owning context is
serialised against the accept/read handler on the listening socket.

The rebind opens the replacement socket before unregistering the old one,
so a bind failure - a typo, or an address that is not local - leaves the
running listener in place and logs an error, rather than leaving the daemon
with no listener at all. Established sessions have their own sockets and
contexts and are unaffected either way.

For the TCP listeners (pptp, sstp) that order is not always possible: Linux
refuses a bind() overlapping a socket in LISTEN state on the same port even
with SO_REUSEADDR, which is exactly the case when moving from 0.0.0.0 to a
specific address. On EADDRINUSE the old listener is released first, and if
the new address still cannot be bound the old one is re-opened. The open
helpers return -errno so the caller can tell this case apart; a failed
re-open leaves the server without a listener, which the context close
handler and later reloads tolerate.

For SSTP only "bind" and "port" are handled here. The TLS material is
already reloaded correctly by ssl_load_config(), which builds a fresh
SSL_CTX and swaps it in for new connections while established sessions keep
the context they were accepted with. A unix-domain socket path is unlinked
after the switch-over rather than before, so the old socket keeps serving
until the new one is ready.

Drive-by fixes in the code being moved:

  - l2tp passed &udp_serv.hnd.fd as the option value to
    setsockopt(SO_REUSEADDR) and SO_NO_CHECK, i.e. the file descriptor
    number rather than a boolean. It happened to work because a valid fd
    is non-zero. It now passes an int set to 1.
  - sstp ran inet_pton(AF_INET6, ...) against the address union before
    deciding which member to use; the union is now zeroed first, so the
    result no longer depends on what a failed parse left behind.
